meta {
  name: Move Item
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

patch {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/items/{{itemId}}/move
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "workphaseId": "{{workphaseId}}",
    "anchorId": "{{anchorId}}",
    "side": "after"
  }
}

docs {
  # Move Item

  Primary shape: `anchorId` + `side` ("before" | "after") — the server
  resolves the actual position against the anchor's true current neighbor.
  Omit `anchorId`/`side` to append to the end of the target scope.

  `workphaseId` alone (no anchor) is a distinct case: it keeps the item's
  existing `position` unchanged rather than appending — position is
  workflow-scoped, so changing only the workphase doesn't invalidate the
  existing value. This is also the shape the MCP `move_item` tool sends.

  No caller names a final position: a request with a `position` is a 400
  that says to name an anchor and side instead (wiki positioning §2).

  ## Hierarchy

  `parentId` (string, or `null` for "make top-level") and `isAttached`
  (boolean) can ride along with any of the above and commit in the same
  transaction — e.g. the board's "drop Card onto Card" gesture sends
  `{ parentId, isAttached: true, anchorId, side }`. Omitting `isAttached`
  keeps the item's current state; nothing attaches by default. Never send
  `projectId`: it's derived from the parent.

  A write the hierarchy rules refuse is a 400. The rules are the wiki's, in
  wiki item-attachment §2a, §4, §5, §7, §8 and §9: the workphase a write
  leaves the item in, the position it resolves, what makes a parent legal,
  the depth cap, and a parent in another project.

  The response is the item row plus, only when a reindex fired, a
  `reindexed: [{id, position}]` collection covering every renumbered item
  (wiki positioning §7).
}
